    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d531">

                <pb facs="1120" n="1080" xml:id="pg1080"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/12–1752: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 531<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the
                        United States Mission at the United Nations</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-12-17T20:02:00-05:00">December 17, 1952—8:02
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Gadel 104. Re Palestine. By direct order of Pres conveyed to Acting Sec tonight,
                    you are instructed to vote against any amendments to Pal res<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d531-f1"/> as it stands and to muster all support you can for res in
                    its present form.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bruce</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d531-f1" n="1" xml:id="d531-f1-content">
                <p>Reference is to the eight-power resolution as revised (U.N. doc.
                    A/AC.61/L.23/Rev.4), adopted by the <hi rendition="#italic">Ad Hoc</hi>
                    Political Committee at its 39th meeting on Dec. 11. The report of the Committee
                    (U.N. doc. A/2310) was considered by the General Assembly at its 405th and 406th
                    plenary meetings on Dec. 18. The draft resolution proposed by the Committee was
                    rejected by a roll-call vote of 24 in favor (including the United States) to 21
                    against, with 15 abstentions. (A/PV.405–406)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
